    <!-- Our story / founder confession -->
    <div class="section-head" id="sec-story" style="margin-top:0"><p class="eyebrow">Our story</p><h2>A confession.</h2></div>
    <div class="story-grid">
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:72ch">
        {%- comment -%}
        UNITS: "17g" below is a DELIBERATE exception to Brand Standards §9 (both metric and
        U.S. customary on every weight reference). Everywhere else on the site now carries
        both. A brew dose is the one weight that is expressed in grams even in America -
        every US espresso recipe reads "18 g in, 36 g out" - so "17 g (0.60 oz)" would read
        as LESS fluent to the exact reader the hero names, "people who love to grind their
        own beans". It is also a sentence in a personal story, not a product spec.
        Same shape as the documented em-dash exception on the regions map. Do not "fix" it
        in a units sweep; if Steve wants it converted, convert it here only.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Founder story revised 2026-09-12 (Steve, refined with Code). Three additions, each paid
        for, so the section grew only 115 -> 133 words against Steve's draft at ~140 (the review had
        asked to halve, not grow, this section): the cupboard contrast ("Italian espresso" that didn't come close -
        the style-vs-place thesis as a personal beat, no argument needed), "whose craft is
        uniquely their own" for the roasters (2026-09-13: "entirely" -> "uniquely", Steve), and "Just Italy, roasted into a bean." Kept on
        purpose: the DECAF - the best specific in the story, even the decaf was that good - and
        the 17g dose (POC15: a brew dose is written in grams even in America; the fluent wink).
        Declined: "No American hype." (the one sentence on the page that pushes the primary
        buyer away, and "no hype" is itself a claim), a 34g yield and "un caff&egrave; double"
        (turns a wink into a recipe, and the word would be doppio), and "find your coffee beans
        here" (the old "find it here" carries two meanings, the coffee and the memory).
        Quotation marks are &ldquo; &rdquo; entities to match this file's &rsquo; convention.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p>I&rsquo;ve fallen in love with Italy, and all things Italian - especially the caff&egrave; culture. As I left Tuscany, a friend handed me a parting gift: a bag of decaf beans. Home again, I ground exactly 17g, pulled a shot (<span class="ita">un caff&egrave;</span>), and was blown away. The &ldquo;Italian espresso&rdquo; in my cupboard didn&rsquo;t come close. So I looked for the roaster online to order more, and found they didn&rsquo;t ship.</p>
        <p>That shot was the inspiration, and it is now our mission: to find Italian roasters whose craft is uniquely their own, and bring their coffee here, unchanged. Just Italy, roasted into a bean. Perhaps you have already discovered Italy and want to rekindle a memory. Maybe you simply want high-quality beans to grind and savor. Either way, I hope you find it here.</p>
        <img class="founder-signature" src="{{ 'ci-signature.png' | asset_url }}" alt="Steve Roberts" width="184" height="78" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        <p class="founder-sign">Steve Roberts, Founder</p>
      </div>
