    <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Our model</p><h2>Curated, never aggregated.</h2></div>
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:72ch">
      {%- comment -%}
        VOICE: this sentence used to read "We have carefully hand-selected a small number of
        roasters whose work represents Italian roasting at its finest." That is the Brand
        Standards' own named anti-pattern, inverted - §9 prints "We hand-pick the world's most
        exclusive coffee artisans" as the example NOT to write, and §6 of CLAUDE.md bans
        "hand-picked" and "world-class" outright. The replacement below is the Standard's
        approved construction, near-verbatim. Do not reintroduce a superlative here: the
        restraint IS the positioning.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        POC30 item 2b (2026-09-12): "Our model" TRIMMED so the model is said once on the page.
        Its old second half - do not roast/grind/blend, air-freight into the US facility, whole
        bean only - is now the three beats under the hero, in twelve words. What replaced it is
        Steve's copy, refined: HOW a roaster gets found (cafes, espresso, ask, the same name
        again, the visit, "Si, we do this together") - the roaster story the review said carries
        the whole trust load, told nowhere else on the site - and the partner line. Kept from
        the old tail on purpose: the roast date on every bag, the one fact the beats do not
        carry and the review's "cheap table-stakes trust". Declined from Steve's draft: the
        tasting-notes parenthetical (interrupts the story; the notes sit on every product card),
        "a quality pattern is detected" (passive, lab register - now "the same name keeps coming
        up"), and two comma splices (spaced dashes, per the semicolon rule). "Si" carries its
        accent and an .ita span, because italic means Italian here and nothing else.
        The first sentence is still the Standard's approved construction - see the VOICE note
        above; do not reintroduce a superlative.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p>We choose a small number of roasters whose work represents the craft of Italian roasting at its best. Choosing means sitting in caf&eacute;s and drinking a great deal of espresso. When a coffee is delicious, we ask about the roaster. When the same name keeps coming up, we go and visit. It begins with the love of a coffee and ends with &ldquo;<span class="ita">S&igrave;</span>, we do this together.&rdquo;</p>
      <p>Our roasters are not vendors - they are partners. They are the masters of roasting and packaging - we are the means of getting their coffee to you, unchanged. The roast date is on every bag we sell.</p>
      <p>Meet the full group on our <button class="inline-link" onclick="showPage('roasters')">Roasters page</button>. Do you have a roaster you&rsquo;d like us to consider? <a class="inline-link" href="mailto:roasters@cremaitalia.com">Email me here.</a></p>
    </div>
