<!-- ============================== OFFERTA (HIDDEN - invitation only) ============================== -->
{%- comment -%}
  POC30 item 4 (2026-09-12, Steve): Offerta is HIDDEN. Nothing on the site links here. The only
  way in is a URL carrying an invitation parameter - ?offer=<anything> - which initOfferEntry()
  in ci-storefront.js reads on load. Leave by any navigation and there is no way back except the
  link that brought you (Back from an Offerta PRODUCT does return here, or the offer could not be
  browsed - Steve accepted that distinction). Nothing is stored in the browser.
  TESTING: append ?offer=test to any preview URL, e.g. http://127.0.0.1:9292/?offer=test
  WHY: all research says a visible markdown shelf teaches customers to wait for the markdown.
  Offerta is a last resort to move product, sold as what it also is - the roaster's coffee,
  the same quality, with less of it left. Not "last chance", not "markdown", no countdown.
  PROD: an UNLISTED collection. Not in any menu; the collection AND each product in it carry
  Shopify's seo.hidden metafield (drops them from the sitemap and site search, adds noindex);
  products excluded from every public collection by tag; the email (Klaviyo, when chosen)
  carries the collection URL. A signed, expiring link needs a backend and is a later option -
  the price sits on the product itself, no code is involved (Standard section 3), so a
  forwarded link costs nothing a recipient could not get by being on the list.
  Same mechanism a wholesale page would use, plus a customer-tag gate.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="page" id="page-offerta">
  <div class="dark-hero"><div class="dark-hero-inner">
    <button class="back-btn" style="color:var(--ci-crema-light)" onclick="goBack()">&larr; Back</button>
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--ci-crema-light)">By invitation</p>
    <h1>Offerta</h1>
    <p class="sub">Great coffee, while it lasts.</p>
    <p>This is the coffee we sell on our shelves, from the same roasters, bought the same way. It is nearer the end of our freshness window than we usually sell it, and it is priced for the time it has left. The quality is the roaster&rsquo;s. The supply is what is running out. You see the roast-date band before you buy.</p>
    <div class="cn"><strong>Sold as-is.</strong> This coffee came off our shelves because its roast date is older than {{ settings.freshness_window_days }} days. By commercial standards it is viable for 12 to 24 months from roast. We offer it by invitation, first come, first served, for a limited time before we donate it to charity, and we say where in the Journal. We will replace a bag that is genuinely defective, but not one you simply wish were fresher.</div>
  </div></div>
  <main class="ci-main">
    <div class="callout">This page is not in our navigation - it is reached by invitation only. If you leave it, the link in your email brings you back.</div>
    <div class="card-grid" id="grid-offerta"></div>
    <div class="afd">Offerta prices are set one coffee at a time, not by a blanket percentage off. An Offerta coffee never appears on its roaster&rsquo;s own page, and subscriber and Founding Member discounts do not apply here.</div>
  </main>
</div>

